===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Wearing a [[disguise]]; dressed in strange or unusual [[clothes]], or taking on a [[changed]] [[appearance]], especially to [[conceal]] one’s [[identity]].
# {{lb|en|of things}} Made to [[appear]] as something other than it is, [[hidden]] in outward form.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|of dress}} [[alter|Altered]] for the sake of [[fashion]]; [[newfangled]] or [[showy]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|of dress, names, etc.}} Serving as a disguise; altered for the sake of [[conceal]]ing one’s identity.
#: {{ux|en|under a '''disguised''' name}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Concealed, [[hidden]], [[out of sight]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[act|Acting]] [[inappropriately]], [[badly]] [[behaved]].
#* {{RQ:Skelton Complete|Hauke|62|lines=20–23|Therefore to make complaynt<br>Of such mysadvysed<br>Parsons and '''dysgysed''',<br>Thys boke we have devysed, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Skelton Complete|Collyn Clout|261|lines=574–576, 579–580|And thus the loselles stryves,<br>And lewdely sayes by Chryst<br>Agaynst the sely preest. {{...}}<br>They mought be better advysed<br>Then to be so '''dysgysed'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|obsolete|by extension}} [[drunk|Drunk]].
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|passage=He was never known to be '''disguised''' with liquor […].|I|4}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1600|author=Thomas Deloney|title=Strange histories, or, Songs and sonnets
|passage=The saylers and the shipmen all, / Through foul excesse of wine, / Were so '''disguised''' that at the sea / They shew'd themselves like swine.}}

